INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - HARVEST NIGHT

A long pine table crowded with cornbread, pickled beets, and a pot of paprika stew. Oil lamps burn though the electricity works. Through the open window, the throb of a combine in the field, working late.

OLEKSANDR (70s, white moustache, suspenders) sits at the head of the table, carving bread with a knife older than his children. His wife MIREILLE (60s, floured apron, silver braid) ladles stew into mismatched bowls. Their son TEDDY (40s, city jacket, gold watch) sits restlessly to the right, thumbing his phone under the table. His sister ROSA (30s, work gloves tucked into her belt, sunburned neck) leans against the stove with her arms folded. At the far end, AUGUSTINE (20s, neighbor boy, cap in his hands) waits to be told where to sit. And in the corner, perched on a milking stool, KIERAN (10, the grandson, scabbed knees, a jar of fireflies) holds the jar in both hands and watches everyone.

Mireille sets a bowl in front of Teddy. He looks at it as if it were an invoice.

OLEKSANDR
Eat.

TEDDY
I'm not hungry.

ROSA
You haven't been hungry in this house in fifteen years.

TEDDY
(quietly, to her)
Don't start.

OLEKSANDR
(at the same time)
Teddy, we have guests.

^ROSA
(at the same time)
Somebody has to start.

Mireille puts the ladle down with a clink. Augustine edges toward a chair and pretends to be fascinated by the wallpaper. Kieran lifts his jar of fireflies to the lamp.

ROSA (CONT'D)
The bank called about the loan again.

TEDDY
I know.

ROSA
You know?

TEDDY
I'm the one who signed it.

Oleksandr's knife stops in the bread. Mireille touches the edge of her apron. Nobody speaks. The combine in the field coughs and goes quiet. Kieran's fireflies blink.

OLEKSANDR
You signed?

TEDDY
For the combine. Papa, I did it for the harvest.

OLEKSANDR
You put the land on that paper.

TEDDY
It's a bridge loan. We pay it off in March.

ROSA
(low)
If the wheat sells.

TEDDY
It will sell.

ROSA
Hail came through Tuesday. Forty acres flattened.

TEDDY
Why am I only hearing about this now?

ROSA
Because you only come when there's something to sign.

Teddy shoves his chair back. Oleksandr raises one hand. The room freezes: Rosa with her arms locked, Teddy half-risen, Mireille clutching the ladle, Augustine wide-eyed in the lamplight, Kieran cradling his glowing jar.

OLEKSANDR
Sit down.

Teddy sits. Oleksandr lays the knife across the loaf, wipes his hands on his trousers, and rises, slow, using the table's edge. He crosses to the window and stands looking out into the dark.

OLEKSANDR (CONT'D)
My father lost this land in forty-six. My brother and I won it back with our hands. It was never ours. It was a loan from the weather.

MIREILLE
Oleksandr.

OLEKSANDR
Let me say it. If the bank takes it, the bank takes it. I'm not afraid of a letter.

ROSA
Papa...

OLEKSANDR
What I am afraid of is this. A table where nobody tells the truth until the harvest is in.

He turns. His eyes find Kieran's.

OLEKSANDR (CONT'D)
Boy. What is in your jar?

KIERAN
Fireflies, Dedo.

OLEKSANDR
How many?

KIERAN
Eleven.

OLEKSANDR
Are they happy?

KIERAN
(considering)
They glow when they're scared.

A silence. Then Mireille begins to laugh, hand over her mouth, and then Rosa, and then, unwillingly, Teddy. Augustine, relieved, sits at last. Oleksandr crosses back to the table, takes Teddy by the shoulder, and sits beside him.

OLEKSANDR
Eat, my son. Tomorrow we count the wheat that's left.

Teddy lifts his spoon. Kieran sets his jar on the table. In the glow of eleven small lights, the family eats.
